+(duration-type)=
+
+### Duration
+
+The {class}`Duration` type converts a duration string into a {class}`datetime.timedelta`. A value is either a whole
+number of seconds, like `120`, or one or more groups of an integer followed by a unit, like `2d`, `1h30m`, or
+`1d2h3m4s`. The units are `d` (days), `h` (hours), `m` (minutes), and `s` (seconds). Each unit may appear at most once,
+in that order, without spaces. Units are case-insensitive, so `45S` is the same as `45s`. Negative numbers and decimals
+are not accepted. A {class}`datetime.timedelta` value, such as a default, is passed through unchanged.
+
+```{eval-rst}
+.. click:example::
+
+    @click.command()
+    @click.option("--timeout", type=click.Duration(), default="30s")
+    def wait(timeout):
+        click.echo(f"Timeout: {timeout}")
+
+.. click:run::
+
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=1h30m'])
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=90'])
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=1.5h'])
+    println()
+    invoke(wait, args=['--help'])
+```
+
